{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Gad, a troop shall troop against him</b>. Rashi explained following the way of Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 49:19): he prophesies that a troop shall troop from him, and that troop shall troop on the heel of his heel, that it will return by the road by which it came, in peace to its land. But the correct interpretation is that it should be like \"to march against the people will he march\" (Habakkuk 3:16) — he says that a troop shall troop against him constantly, that he will have many wars and a troop will raid against him in his land, and he shall troop on its heel, that he will prevail over it and pursue it, and they will turn back on the heel in shame. He praised them for might and victory over all who come against them in battle. And this is like the matter of the blessing of Moses our teacher upon them (Deuteronomy 33:20): \"Blessed be He who enlarges Gad; he dwells like a lioness,\" because since he inherited a broad and very great land and it is across the Jordan, the troops of Ammon and Moab, his evil neighbors who claim the inheritance, would come against them constantly and raid against them, and he dwells like a lioness on its prey — from their voice he does not fear and from their multitudes he is not dismayed. And this is a fine interpretation that I learned from the Jerusalem Talmud, which they said in tractate Sotah (8:11): \"Gad, a troop shall troop against him\" — a host comes mustering, and he musters against it. To say that the host comes to gather force and to muster against him, and he troops against them and brings his troops into their land. And perhaps the prophet alludes to the war of Jephthah the Gileadite with the children of Ammon, for the children of Gad inherited all the cities of Gilead and half the land of the children of Ammon, and the children of Ammon were constantly warring against the people of Gilead, and he nearly overwhelmed them and struck them and their cities a very great blow (Judges 11:33), and that event was a great sign; the prophet mentioned it just as he mentioned the matter of Samson."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {0 "The phrase עֲקֵב עַל עֲקֵבוֹ is rendered literally 'on the heel of his heel'; Ramban's play on עָקֵב/עֵקֶב (heel/return) is preserved without resolution."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:47:30.859692642Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}